  "leaderboardRowSemantics": "{count, plural, zero{no points} one{1 point} other{{digits} points}}, {initials}, rank {rank}{isReader, select, true{, that is you} other{}}",
  "@leaderboardRowSemantics": {
    "description": "The screen-reader sentence for one ranked row.\n\nA REAL ICU PLURAL, and the reason this key exists rather than four separate strings. A row is four numbers and a monogram; a screen reader announcing \"3, PG, 80, points\" gives a reader four fragments and no sentence, so this composes one. The plural is over `count` with the digits interpolated separately, which is the same shape `readingCaptionFor` uses.\n\n`isReader` is a `select`, not a second plural: \"that is you\" is a clause, not a quantity, and folding it into the plural would multiply the arms for no gain.\n\nNEVER A NAME. `initials` is the monogram and nothing else can reach this string, which is the accessibility half of `LeaderboardRow` having no `fullName` — a voice-over user must not be able to hear a name the screen cannot show.\n\n`rank` and `digits` are **String** while `count` is **int**, and that split is load-bearing rather than fussy: `count` is what the plural selects on, and `digits` is what the reader sees. Passing the numeral twice means the shape does not have to change when a locale's digits are not ASCII — the Arabic arm renders U+0660…U+0669, and `intl`'s formatting would otherwise be a second source of truth about numerals. `readingCaptionFor` does the same thing.",
    "placeholders": {
      "count": {
        "type": "int",
        "example": "80"
      },
      "digits": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "80"
      },
      "initials": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "PG"
      },
      "rank": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "3"
      },
      "isReader": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "true"
      }
    }
  },
  "@leaderboardTitle": {
    "description": "App bar / section title for `/leaderboard`. The cohort's ranking over a chosen window."
  },
  "@leaderboardWindowWeekly": {
    "description": "Segmented-control option for `LeaderboardWindow.weekly`.\n\nNOT the enum's wire code. The wire sends `weekly`, `monthly`, `all_time`; those are identifiers in `leaderboard_board.dart`, not words to put in front of a reader. `all_time` in particular would need a space and a lowercase 't' to read as English."
  },
  "@leaderboardWindowMonthly": {
    "description": "Segmented-control option for `LeaderboardWindow.monthly`. See `leaderboardWindowWeekly` for why this is not the wire code."
  },
  "@leaderboardWindowAllTime": {
    "description": "Segmented-control option for `LeaderboardWindow.allTime`. See `leaderboardWindowWeekly` for why this is not the wire code `all_time`."
  },
  "@leaderboardEmptyTitle": {
    "description": "Shown when the cohort has nobody ranked.\n\nA REAL STATE, NOT AN ERROR. Measured against the live endpoint: `{\"group_id\":3,\"window\":\"weekly\",\"total_ranked\":0,\"my_standing\":null,\"leaderboard\":[]}`. Scores only enter Redis via `leaderboardService.recordScore`, called from `submissions.service.ts:105` on a *successful* submission, and submission cannot succeed against the memory fallback because the schemas require UUIDs the fallback does not mint. So the empty board is currently the ONLY reachable state in development.\n\nThis is why the copy explains what will fill it instead of apologising for a failure."
  },
  "@leaderboardEmptyBody": {
    "description": "Body under `leaderboardEmptyTitle`. Says what will populate the list, because the reader cannot tell whether the cohort is new or the app is broken."
  },
  "@leaderboardYourRank": {
    "description": "Label on the reader's own row. NOT the reader's name and NOT their initials — the top bar already shows both, and repeating the initials next to the flame would be a second copy of the same identity.\n\nA reader must find their own row in a list of other children's monograms without their name appearing anywhere, which is the whole point of `LeaderboardRow` having no `fullName`."
  },
  "@leaderboardUnranked": {
    "description": "Shown instead of a position when `LeaderboardStanding.rank` is `null`.\n\nNEVER AN ARITHMETIC RESULT. `rank: null` with `score: 0` is the server's measured answer for a child who has not scored. Rendering it as `0` or `1` would put a fabricated position on someone who has none — the defect `LeaderboardStanding`'s doc is named for."
  },
  "@leaderboardScoreLabel": {
    "description": "Unit caption under a score. Lowercase and plural-invariant because the unit is fixed and the number is not; 'pts' was rejected as too terse to read as a word in Arabic, where a two-letter abbreviation carries no meaning."
  },
  "@leaderboardOpenFull": {
    "description": "Link from Home's compact card to `/leaderboard`.\n\nPhrased as an action rather than a count, because the card cannot honestly say how many there are — `cohortSize` is `null` whenever the request's `limit` bound. 'See all' stays true whether there are four children or four hundred."
  },
  "@leaderboardCardTitle": {
    "description": "Title on Home's compact card.\n\nFIXED TO THE WEEK on purpose. The card always requests `LeaderboardWindow.weekly` — `LoadLeaderboardPreview` takes no window — so a title that varied with the data would be claiming a precision the request does not have."
  },
